export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","addon":"pool","name":"Pool generator","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"pool-start","scope":"addon:pool","title":"Pool generator: getting started","summary":"Make a swimming pool or a fountain from its length, width and depth: the concrete shell, steps, tiles, coping and paving, the filter, pump, pipes and a machine room, with quantities and an IFC file. A drawing aid, not a design.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["pool","swimming pool","fountain","L-shaped pool","round pool","freeform pool","benches","gate","rebar","bar schedule","feet and inches","report binder","concrete shell","tiles","paving","coping","filter","pump","pipes","machine room","plant room","skimmer","overflow","turnover","IFC","quantities","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"shapes-steps-benches-and-the-gate","text":"Shapes, steps, benches and the gate","level":2},{"id":"reinforcement-as-bars","text":"Reinforcement as bars","level":2},{"id":"pipes-and-the-report-binder","text":"Pipes and the report binder","level":2},{"id":"where-the-figures-come-from","text":"Where the figures come from","level":2},{"id":"specs-are-side-add-ons","text":"Specs are side add-ons","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-do","text":"What it does not do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESwitch the add-on on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E, then open the \u003Cstrong\u003EPool generator\u003C/strong\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EShape\u003C/strong\u003E, pick the \u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E (metric, or US feet and inches) and the \u003Cstrong\u003EShape\u003C/strong\u003E (rectangle, L-shaped, round or oval, or freeform from your own corners), then type the length (A), the width (B) and the water depth at the shallow and the deep end. Choose a swimming pool or a fountain, outdoors or indoors, private or public, and skimmers or an overflow channel all round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the \u003Cstrong\u003EResult\u003C/strong\u003E: the plan and the long section, the water volume, the circulation flow, the filter, pump and pipe sizes, and the quantities (concrete, formwork, steel, tiles, coping, paving, pipes).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ESave as an IFC file\u003C/strong\u003E for the 3D model, or \u003Cstrong\u003ESave quantities (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E for the figures.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shapes-steps-benches-and-the-gate\"\u003EShapes, steps, benches and the gate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EL-shaped\u003C/strong\u003E cuts a rectangle of the cut-out length and width from the deep end&#039;s far corner. \u003Cstrong\u003ERound or oval\u003C/strong\u003E fits an oval in the length and width (a circle when they are equal). \u003Cstrong\u003EFreeform\u003C/strong\u003E takes your corners, one pair per corner: \u003Ccode\u003E0,0; 9000,0; 9000,3000; 5000,5000; 0,4000\u003C/code\u003E (feet and inches work in US units: \u003Ccode\u003E30&#039;,0\u003C/code\u003E). The floor always falls in one slope from the shallow end (the smallest x) to the deep end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe sides are numbered on the plan. \u003Cstrong\u003ESteps on side\u003C/strong\u003E puts the entry steps on that side (0 keeps them at the shallow end); \u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle of the steps along the side\u003C/strong\u003E moves them along it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBenches\u003C/strong\u003E are one line each: side, where it starts along the side, and its length. The seat is the depth you set under the water.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebarrier\u003C/strong\u003E height, a \u003Cstrong\u003Egate\u003C/strong\u003E of the width you set leaves an opening in the barrier on the side you pick. Self-closing and latching hardware is the local rule&#039;s: check it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reinforcement-as-bars\"\u003EReinforcement as bars\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cstrong\u003EBars\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EReinforcement\u003C/strong\u003E for a bar layout and a bar schedule: one layer each way in the walls and the floor, spaced from the steel ratio and your bar diameter, never wider than the largest spacing (the spec&#039;s when it has one). The lap, the floor leg and the 12 m stock length are starting figures, not from a standard. It is a schedule to estimate from, not a reinforcement design.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pipes-and-the-report-binder\"\u003EPipes and the report binder\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pipes in the IFC file are one connected network: straight lengths, bends and junctions, with ports on every pipe, fitting, skimmer, inlet, drain, pump and filter, and each pair that meets connected. \u003Cstrong\u003ECalculation page (.md)\u003C/strong\u003E saves the pool as one page; in SuperCad Studio the same page goes into the project&#039;s report binder under Calculations while the Pool generator tab is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-figures-come-from\"\u003EWhere the figures come from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThicknesses, cover, steel, turnover time, filter rate, pipe velocities and skimmer spacing are set by norms. The generator holds \u003Cstrong\u003Eno norm values\u003C/strong\u003E. Leave those boxes empty and each one is filled:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Efrom the \u003Cstrong\u003Espec\u003C/strong\u003E you picked, when a spec add-on is switched on and has a value for it, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Efrom a \u003Cstrong\u003Estarting figure\u003C/strong\u003E, which is a round number so that something is drawn. A starting figure is not from any standard, and the box says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType your own figure in a box to overrule either. The result then says when your figure breaks the spec.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"specs-are-side-add-ons\"\u003ESpecs are side add-ons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd \u003Cstrong\u003EPool spec: Europe\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EPool spec: United States\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E, then pick it under \u003Cstrong\u003ESpec\u003C/strong\u003E. The table under the picker lists every rule in the spec with its standard, its clause and how we read it: in the standard itself, in a document that quotes the standard, or not at all. Check each value against your own copy of the standard and its national annex before you rely on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-do\"\u003EWhat it does not do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt does not design the pool. Nothing is checked for strength, ground water, uplift, cracking, entrapment or electrical safety. The steel is an estimate from a ratio, or a simple bar schedule if you ask for bars, never a reinforcement design. The machine room pipes are laid out square by fixed offsets and not checked for clashes, and valves are not drawn. The pump head is your own figure unless you choose &quot;Calculated from the routed pipes&quot;: then it is the friction in the routed pipes (Hazen-Williams), the bends and your filter-and-valves figure. The roughness, bend factor and equipment loss are starting figures, not from a standard, and the height of the pump above the water, a dirty filter and valve throttling are not counted. Filter, motor and pipe sizes are generic size steps, not a maker&#039;s catalogue. A licensed engineer must design and check the pool.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Switch the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons, then open the Pool generator tab. Under Shape, pick the Units (metric, or US feet and inches) and the Shape (rectangle, L-shaped, round or oval, or freeform from your own corners), then type the length (A), the width (B) and the water depth at the shallow and the deep end. Choose a swimming pool or a fountain, outdoors or indoors, private or public, and skimmers or an overflow channel all round. Read the Result: the plan and the long section, the water volume, the circulation flow, the filter, pump and pipe sizes, and the quantities (concrete, formwork, steel, tiles, coping, paving, pipes). Press Save as an IFC file for the 3D model, or Save quantities (CSV) for the figures. Shapes, steps, benches and the gate L-shaped cuts a rectangle of the cut-out length and width from the deep end's far corner. Round or oval fits an oval in the length and width (a circle when they are equal). Freeform takes your corners, one pair per corner: 0,0; 9000,0; 9000,3000; 5000,5000; 0,4000 (feet and inches work in US units: 30',0). The floor always falls in one slope from the shallow end (the smallest x) to the deep end. The sides are numbered on the plan. Steps on side puts the entry steps on that side (0 keeps them at the shallow end); Middle of the steps along the side moves them along it. Benches are one line each: side, where it starts along the side, and its length. The seat is the depth you set under the water. With a barrier height, a gate of the width you set leaves an opening in the barrier on the side you pick. Self-closing and latching hardware is the local rule's: check it. Reinforcement as bars Pick Bars under Reinforcement for a bar layout and a bar schedule: one layer each way in the walls and the floor, spaced from the steel ratio and your bar diameter, never wider than the largest spacing (the spec's when it has one). The lap, the floor leg and the 12 m stock length are starting figures, not from a standard. It is a schedule to estimate from, not a reinforcement design. Pipes and the report binder The pipes in the IFC file are one connected network: straight lengths, bends and junctions, with ports on every pipe, fitting, skimmer, inlet, drain, pump and filter, and each pair that meets connected. Calculation page (.md) saves the pool as one page; in SuperCad Studio the same page goes into the project's report binder under Calculations while the Pool generator tab is open. Where the figures come from Thicknesses, cover, steel, turnover time, filter rate, pipe velocities and skimmer spacing are set by norms. The generator holds no norm values. Leave those boxes empty and each one is filled: from the spec you picked, when a spec add-on is switched on and has a value for it, or from a starting figure, which is a round number so that something is drawn. A starting figure is not from any standard, and the box says so. Type your own figure in a box to overrule either. The result then says when your figure breaks the spec. Specs are side add-ons Add Pool spec: Europe or Pool spec: United States in Settings › Add-ons, then pick it under Spec. The table under the picker lists every rule in the spec with its standard, its clause and how we read it: in the standard itself, in a document that quotes the standard, or not at all. Check each value against your own copy of the standard and its national annex before you rely on it. What it does not do It does not design the pool. Nothing is checked for strength, ground water, uplift, cracking, entrapment or electrical safety. The steel is an estimate from a ratio, or a simple bar schedule if you ask for bars, never a reinforcement design. The machine room pipes are laid out square by fixed offsets and not checked for clashes, and valves are not drawn. The pump head is your own figure unless you choose \"Calculated from the routed pipes\": then it is the friction in the routed pipes (Hazen-Williams), the bends and your filter-and-valves figure. The roughness, bend factor and equipment loss are starting figures, not from a standard, and the height of the pump above the water, a dirty filter and valve throttling are not counted. Filter, motor and pipe sizes are generic size steps, not a maker's catalogue. A licensed engineer must design and check the pool."},{"slug":"pool-bimcraft","scope":"addon:pool","title":"Pool generator: the pool in your model or drawing","summary":"Place the pool in a BimCraft model as elements with properties and quantities, or draw its plan and section in CadCraft. Both save an IFC file from the panel.","section":"tasks","order":2,"keywords":["pool","place","IFC","elements","slab","wall","covering","pump","filter","pipe","plan","section","layers","POOL","undo"],"headings":[{"id":"in-bimcraft","text":"In BimCraft","level":2},{"id":"in-cadcraft","text":"In CadCraft","level":2},{"id":"the-ifc-file","text":"The IFC file","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"in-bimcraft\"\u003EIn BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003EPlace in the model\u003C/strong\u003E. The pool is written on the model&#039;s first level as elements: the floor slab and walls, the steps, the tile coverings, coping and paving, the barrier, the machine room, each filter and pump, the drains, skimmers and inlets, and every pipe. Each element has a \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Pool\u003C/code\u003E property set and base quantities, so it schedules and saves in the IFC file like anything else. The top of the coping stands at the level&#039;s height.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt is one step: \u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E takes the whole pool back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlacing again replaces the pool \u003Cstrong\u003Eof the same name\u003C/strong\u003E. Give a second pool another name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPosition\u003C/strong\u003E moves the pool&#039;s shallow-end corner from the level&#039;s origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove the pool\u003C/strong\u003E takes the elements of that name out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-cadcraft\"\u003EIn CadCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003EDraw plan and section\u003C/strong\u003E. The plan and the long section are drawn as polylines, lines, text and dimensions on \u003Ccode\u003EPOOL-\u003C/code\u003E layers, in the drawing&#039;s own units. They are ordinary entities: they plot and export to DXF and DWG, and they do not follow later changes to the pool. Press \u003Cstrong\u003ERemove the pool drawing\u003C/strong\u003E, change the pool, and draw again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-ifc-file\"\u003EThe IFC file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESave as an IFC file\u003C/strong\u003E writes an IFC4 file of the same pool from either app: one element per part in its own class, with colours, properties, quantities and materials. Open it in BimCraft or any IFC viewer. The pipes are connected: every pipe, bend, junction, skimmer, inlet, drain, pump and filter has its ports, and each pair that meets is connected. With \u003Cstrong\u003EBars\u003C/strong\u003E picked, the bar marks are in the file as reinforcing bars. \u003Cstrong\u003EPlace in the model\u003C/strong\u003E writes the same ports and connections into BimCraft&#039;s model.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"In BimCraft Press Place in the model. The pool is written on the model's first level as elements: the floor slab and walls, the steps, the tile coverings, coping and paving, the barrier, the machine room, each filter and pump, the drains, skimmers and inlets, and every pipe. Each element has a CadCraft_Pool property set and base quantities, so it schedules and saves in the IFC file like anything else. The top of the coping stands at the level's height. It is one step: Undo takes the whole pool back. Placing again replaces the pool of the same name. Give a second pool another name. Position moves the pool's shallow-end corner from the level's origin. Remove the pool takes the elements of that name out. In CadCraft Press Draw plan and section. The plan and the long section are drawn as polylines, lines, text and dimensions on POOL- layers, in the drawing's own units. They are ordinary entities: they plot and export to DXF and DWG, and they do not follow later changes to the pool. Press Remove the pool drawing, change the pool, and draw again. The IFC file Save as an IFC file writes an IFC4 file of the same pool from either app: one element per part in its own class, with colours, properties, quantities and materials. Open it in BimCraft or any IFC viewer. The pipes are connected: every pipe, bend, junction, skimmer, inlet, drain, pump and filter has its ports, and each pair that meets is connected. With Bars picked, the bar marks are in the file as reinforcing bars. Place in the model writes the same ports and connections into BimCraft's model."}]};
